WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.729 --> 00:00:10.151
more than 120 years, the Statue of Liberty has stood at the entrance to New York Harbor as

2
00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:13.372
a beacon of hope for people coming to the United States by ship.

3
00:00:13.972 --> 00:00:20.754
Although most people refer to it as the Statue of Liberty or Lady Liberty, the statue's full name

4
00:00:21.034 --> 00:00:22.795
is Liberty Enlightening the World.

5
00:00:23.435 --> 00:00:31.192
The name recalls the statue's original purpose: from 1886 to 1902, it served as a lighthouse for boats

6
00:00:31.352 --> 00:00:32.613
entering New York Harbor.

7
00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:41.139
Immigrants from many countries of the world—Ireland, Germany, France, and England, to name just a few—were welcomed to

8
00:00:41.159 --> 00:00:46.663
the land of opportunity by the light of Lady Liberty's torch, which can be seen from almost forty

9
00:00:46.723 --> 00:00:47.604
kilometers away.

10
00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:54.049
One of the most recognizable symbols in the world, the Statue of Liberty has come to represent the

11
00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:59.674
principles on which the United States of America was founded: freedom and opportunity.

12
00:01:00.715 --> 00:01:06.660
Lady Liberty stands 46 meters tall, on top of a pedestal that adds another 47 meters to her

13
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:12.464
height. With the exception of the steel framework (which was designed by Gustave Eiffel, the man who built

14
00:01:12.484 --> 00:01:16.488
the famous Eiffel Tower in Paris), the entire monument is made of copper.

15
00:01:17.224 --> 00:01:21.807
It is located on Liberty Island, which lies in New York Harbor near the mouth of the Hudson

16
00:01:21.847 --> 00:01:26.850
River. Prior to the erection of the Statue of Liberty, the island was called Bedloe's Island.

17
00:01:27.371 --> 00:01:34.875
It was officially renamed Liberty Island in 1956; nine years later the neighboring Ellis Island became part of

18
00:01:34.915 --> 00:01:36.737
the Statue of Liberty National Monument.

19
00:01:37.377 --> 00:01:41.940
Although it represents America and its values, the statue itself was not created there.

20
00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:48.039
It was designed and built in France as a gesture of international friendship and cooperation between the two

21
00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:54.222
countries. The French gave the statue to the United States as a gift to mark the hundredth anniversary

22
00:01:54.262 --> 00:01:57.024
of the signing of the American Declaration of Independence.

23
00:01:58.064 --> 00:02:02.607
The French sculptor Frédéric-Auguste Bartholdi was hired to design the sculpture.

24
00:02:03.387 --> 00:02:10.711
He decided to fashion it after the Roman goddess Libertas, who represented liberty, to symbolize America's commitment to

25
00:02:10.751 --> 00:02:16.950
the rights of life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness outlined by Thomas Jefferson in the Declaration of

26
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:25.134
Independence. Bartholdi designed Lady Liberty so her left hand holds a tablet that reads, in Roman numerals, "July

27
00:02:25.154 --> 00:02:32.097
IV MDCCLXXVI," which is the date that the American colonies declared their independence from Britain.

28
00:02:32.837 --> 00:02:38.720
Bartholdi also placed broken shackles at the feet of the statue to represent freedom from tyranny and oppression.

29
00:02:39.392 --> 00:02:44.715
The seven spikes in the crown symbolize the world's seven seas and seven continents.

30
00:02:45.716 --> 00:02:51.639
While Bartholdi and the French were building the statue, it was up to Americans to erect the pedestal

31
00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:56.202
on which the "classical, yet severe and calm" statue would stand.

32
00:02:56.842 --> 00:03:03.834
The Americans were having difficulty raising money for the pedestal, so the newspaper publisher Joseph Pulitzer used the

33
00:03:03.874 --> 00:03:10.618
editorial section of his newspapers to publish articles criticizing people who were not contributing to the fund-raising efforts.

34
00:03:11.259 --> 00:03:17.483
This tactic worked, and by 1885 enough money had been raised to finance the pedestal's construction.

35
00:03:18.343 --> 00:03:24.767
Lady Liberty's right arm, which holds the torch, was finished first and sent to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, in 1876,

36
00:03:26.188 --> 00:03:30.430
where it was displayed for the hundredth anniversary of the signing of the Declaration of Independence.

37
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:36.372
The arm was later shipped to New York and exhibited in Madison Square until the rest of the

38
00:03:36.412 --> 00:03:37.292
monument was done.

39
00:03:37.973 --> 00:03:43.435
The French completed the rest of the statue in 1884, but since its pedestal was not yet ready,

40
00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:45.256
the 225-ton gift remained in France until 1885.

41
00:03:45.276 --> 00:03:48.857
It was then dismantled into 350 pieces, packed into 214 crates, and shipped to New York City.

42
00:03:56.508 --> 00:03:59.530
It took four months to completely reassemble the statue.

43
00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.551
When it was finished, U.S.

44
00:04:01.611 --> 00:04:07.775
president Grover Cleveland traveled to New York City to dedicate the monument on October 28, 1886.

45
00:04:08.735 --> 00:04:14.598
The symbolism of the monument inspired poet Emma Lazarus to write a poem comparing the Statue of Liberty

46
00:04:14.879 --> 00:04:16.259
to the Colossus of Rhodes.

47
00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:22.643
The Colossus of Rhodes, a huge statue of Helios, the Greek god of the sun and illumination, was

48
00:04:22.743 --> 00:04:24.244
one of the Seven Wonders of the World.

49
00:04:24.968 --> 00:04:31.071
Lazarus' lines will forever be associated with the Statue of Liberty in the minds of Americans: Give me

50
00:04:31.111 --> 00:04:39.136
your tired, your poor, Your huddled masses yearning to breathe free, The wretched refuse of your teeming shore.

51
00:04:39.976 --> 00:04:46.019
Send these, the homeless, tempest-tost to me, I lift my lamp beside the golden door!

52
00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:53.203
In 1903, her poem, "The New Colossus," was engraved on a plaque and mounted on the inner wall

53
00:04:53.343 --> 00:04:54.504
of the monument's pedestal.

54
00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:01.644
The statue was badly damaged in 1916 when a nearby munitions plant in New Jersey blew up in

55
00:05:01.684 --> 00:05:04.006
what has been called the Black Tom Explosion.

56
00:05:04.726 --> 00:05:09.749
The resulting damage to the torch was so extensive that this part had to be closed to visitors.

57
00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:16.493
Unfortunately the repairs were not done well, and the torch leaked when it rained or snowed; moisture then

58
00:05:16.533 --> 00:05:20.376
got into the rest of the monument, causing severe rusting and corrosion.

59
00:05:21.152 --> 00:05:27.438
The damage and neglect over the years meant that extensive renovations were needed before the statue's 1986 centennial.

60
00:05:28.138 --> 00:05:34.664
During these renovations American and French workers together created a new torch made of copper and covered in

61
00:05:34.684 --> 00:05:37.807
gold leaf to replace the damaged glass and metal one.

62
00:05:38.787 --> 00:05:44.893
After the attacks on the World Trade Center and the Pentagon on September 11, 2001, Liberty Island was

63
00:05:44.973 --> 00:05:46.114
closed for three months.

64
00:05:46.854 --> 00:05:51.159
The Statue of Liberty, however, wasn't reopened to the public for nearly three years.

65
00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:56.945
The crown was closed permanently because there is no way to quickly evacuate visitors in case of an

66
00:05:56.985 --> 00:06:03.492
emergency. Time-stamped tickets are required for those who want to enter the monument itself and experience the view

67
00:06:03.592 --> 00:06:05.814
from the tenth-floor pedestal observatory.

68
00:06:06.555 --> 00:06:11.200
Security measures for the Statue of Liberty have also been increased since the 9/11 tragedy.

69
00:06:11.988 --> 00:06:14.969
Visitors have to go through security screenings like those at U.S.

70
00:06:15.009 --> 00:06:19.091
airports before they board the ferry to either Ellis Island or Liberty Island.

71
00:06:20.072 --> 00:06:23.413
Many have suggested that America's values are changing for the worse.

72
00:06:24.193 --> 00:06:29.516
They cite the limited access to one of the nation's most enduring symbols of tolerance as evidence.

73
00:06:30.356 --> 00:06:34.798
The monument and what it represents have faced many challenges over the years.

74
00:06:35.638 --> 00:06:41.588
Yet the Statue of Liberty continues to stand in New York Harbor, welcoming those who seek refuge in

75
00:06:41.648 --> 00:06:46.798
America and reminding the world of the values and principles on which America was built.
